<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ife</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ife is an ancient city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, regarded as the spiritual birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> and renowned for its bronze and terracotta sculpture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> traditions link the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a> to a prince from Ife. This connection is part of the foundation history of the Benin Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ife.jpg" alt="Ife" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oramfe · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ile_Ife_aerial_scape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…first phase of the great earthwork walls and moats of Benin City — among the largest earthworks built before the mechanical era — and, by tradition, with introducing bronze-casting through a master craftsman, Iguegha, sent from <b>Ife</b>, beginning the artistic heritage for which the kingdom would become famous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ife</b> is called the city of 401 deities: the Ooni is counted the 401st orisha, the only one that speaks, and traditional worshippers are said to celebrate a festival of one deity on every day of the year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>They show that a society in the forest zone of West Africa produced sophisticated metalwork centuries before European contact, in a tradition distinct from the later ones of <b>Ife</b> and Benin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sacred centre is Ile-<b>Ife</b>, which Yoruba tradition regards as the place where the world was created.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heads have been compared with the terracottas and copper-alloy heads of <b>Ife</b>, made between about the 12th and 15th centuries CE, and with the art of Benin, and some writers have suggested that peoples of present-day Nigeria descend in part from the Nok people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese folk religion and the religion of the Yoruba, whose sacred centre is <b>Ife</b>, grew over many centuries among towns and lineages, with no founder or fixed date.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
